research_rationale:
EO 13762 of January 13, 2017 (82 FR 7619) set an order of succession for the office of Attorney General (section 1), exceptions (section 2), revoked EO 13557 (section 3) and added a no-rights provision (section 4). EO 13775 of February 9, 2017 (82 FR 10697) enacted a replacement succession list and in its section 3 states: "Executive Order 13762 of January 
13, 2017, is revoked." Section 1 of EO 13762 runs only until at least one named senior officer can act, a standing contingency that does not end the order. The held corpus holds no earlier revocation or amendment of EO 13762; the first and only end is February 9, 2017.
Member checks: {"earlier_end_check":"Own terms: section 1 operates only during a vacancy and 'until such time as at least one of the officers mentioned above is able to perform the functions and duties of that office', a recurring contingency, not a sunset. Searched held texts by the order's number: only EO 13775 and the order's own copies cite it. No earlier revocation, supersession or amendment is held.","independent_command_check":"Section 3 of EO 13762 (revocation of EO 13557) is a one-time self-executing command and is treated in the clause reason and savings; section 4 is a no-rights statement. Neither is an independent continuing command that outlived the revocation.","savings_check":"The surviving consequence is the revocation of EO 13557; EO 13775 does not reinstate it. The order of succession itself is replaced by EO 13775.","scope_check":"Inventory is 10 scopes (a lead paragraph, sections 1 to 4 and their subsections) taken from 82 FR 7619-7620. EO 13775 section 3 revokes the whole order, so every scope ends on the same date."}
